@pohly @yliaog 
> @pohly commented [4 days ago](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/133488#issuecomment-3197978535)
I think it is a known issue that extended resources remain forever in a node status, even after uninstalling the corresponding Device Plugin. cc @klueska for conformation.

I tried to modify Kubelet not to report local node extended resources with Allocatable/Capacity 0 and it worked just fine. However, I suspect it's implemented this way in purpose to distinguish between local node resources and cluster-level resources. 
@ffromani @swatisehgal @SergeyKanzhelev Is it correct assumption?

@pohly 
> However, they should be reported with zero capacity. 

That shouldn't be hard to do by modifying Kubelet code, but we need to hear device plugins maintainers opinion first.

@bart0sh the problem it causes is: scheduling will fail for the node, even when device plugin driver is uninstalled, and DRA drvier has been installed.

we can fix it on the scheduler side by leaving `allocatable` > 0 to DP, and `allocatable` == 0, or not present at all to DRA. the problem i see is it may cause application operators confusion, they see their pods are scheduled to a node with `allocatable` == 0. It could be better if it can be fixed by cluster admin. that said, i'm fine with the scheduler side fix.

for the case that `allocatable` == 0 and `capacity` > 0, if it is just due to DP driver temporary down, then there won't be DRA devices on the node, then scheduler won't be able to schedule a pod to the node in that case.

I am patching the pod under the deployment. And yes the command that I used was a simple
```
kubectl patch pod <pod_name> -p '[{"op": "remove", "path": "/metadata/labels/<label>"}]' --type=json


<omit other lines>
-                              AppArmorProfile: &core.AppArmorProfile{Type: "Localhost", LocalhostProfile: &"<profile>"},
+                              AppArmorProfile: nil,
```

Directly patching the deployment template is fine
```
kubectl patch deployment <deployment> -p '[{"op": "remove", "path": "/spec/template/metadata/labels/<label>"}]' --type=json
Warning: spec.template.metadata.annotations[container.apparmor.security.beta.kubernetes.io/<container>]: deprecated since v1.30; use the "appArmorProfile" field instead
deployment.apps/<deployment> patched
```
But I can't add the appArmorProfile in using `patch`
```
➜  ~ kubectl patch deployment <deployment> \
  --type strategic \
  -p '{
    "spec": {
      "template": {
        "spec": {
          "containers": [{
            "name": "<container>",
            "securityContext": {
              "appArmorProfile": {
                "type": "Localhost",
                "localhostProfile": "<profile?"
              }
            }
          }]
        }
      }
    }
  }'

deployment.apps/<deployment> patched
```
However if I describe the deployment again, nothing was updated. I raised another issue for this: https://github.com/kubernetes/kubectl/issues/1764

